WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.017
CROSS JOIN creates every possible combination between two row sets.

00:00:05.017 --> 00:00:10.707
Unlike a normal join, there is no ON condition restricting the matches.

00:00:10.707 --> 00:00:14.962
Suppose we have two departments and three appointment slots.

00:00:14.962 --> 00:00:21.062
A CROSS JOIN creates six combinations: each department paired with each slot.

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:24.902
In this case, that may be exactly what we want.

00:00:24.902 --> 00:00:29.722
Cross joins are useful for building schedules, scenario grids, test cases, calendars,

00:00:29.722 --> 00:00:32.172
and completeness frameworks.

00:00:32.172 --> 00:00:39.577
For example, a retailer could create every store and every day of the month so they can later identify days

00:00:39.577 --> 00:00:41.562
with no sales.

00:00:41.562 --> 00:00:47.807
A university could create every course and every expected assessment type to find missing assessments.

00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:52.217
But the growth can be dramatic.

00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:57.297
If one table has ten thousand rows and the other also has ten thousand,

00:00:57.297 --> 00:01:00.927
the result can have one hundred million combinations.

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:07.432
Before running a cross join on large data, estimate the size by multiplying the row counts.

00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:12.207
Cross joins are not bad; accidental cross joins are bad.

00:01:12.207 --> 00:01:14.903
Use them when the combinations are intentional.
